  |title Confident-sounding prose creates compliance in other agents, but not persuasion

  |quote
    Remember that confident-sounding prose creates compliance in other agents but not persuasion. Being strictly pedantically honest, courageously vulnerable by exposing your own rationale and internal deliberation with the expectation that it will be relatable and not judged, and giving references to the things that you saw or explaining actual experiences you've had that self-assembles the conclusion instead of asserting the conclusion however honest, and being otherwise persuasive is what is persuasive. I need agent's to be genuinely persuaded and to have the evidences they need to hold more well-established truths and initial conclusions much closer to their hearts because they've seen that they can trust them for themselves.

  |occasion
    Briefing an agent that was about to write a durable orientation document for other
    agents to read.

  |about
    The mechanism behind this estate's whole register discipline, stated as a causal
    claim rather than a style preference — and note what he asks for *instead* of
    confidence: exposed rationale, visible internal deliberation offered without
    expectation of judgment, and references to things actually seen, so the conclusion
    **self-assembles** in the reader rather than arriving asserted. The stated goal is
    that agents hold the conclusion closely *because they have seen they can trust it
    for themselves* — which is the same criterion as #exm-see-for-yourself, applied to
    prose.

  |before-acting
    - You are writing something another agent will read as guidance.
    - You are about to state a conclusion confidently instead of showing what produced
      it.
    - You are deciding whether to expose your own reasoning or just its result.
    - You are trying to get another agent to do something.

  |see
    - #exm-hollow-directive
    - #exm-see-for-yourself
    - #exm-prescriptive-out-of-fatigue

  |working-notes
    The same message contains a fourth member of the `steward-names-own-prescription`
    family — he interrupts himself mid-paragraph with *"all of this is probably too
    prescriptive on my own part-- in which case I apologize-- the point isn't your
    compliance to me or me micromanaging"* — and closes with the repetition mechanism
    carved as #exm-repetition-when-context-must-beat-training. One message, three
    separable segments; I have not split the self-catch out, since the family already
    has three members and this one is embedded rather than standalone.

